  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (through Monday)...\n\nHigh pressure and south/southwest flow will prevail the next few\ndays as an upper low sits just off the California coast with high\npressure over our region. Temps will remain seasonably warm with \nhigh temperatures mainly in the 60s and 70s, warmest on Thursday. \nLow temperatures during this time will be in the 30s to lower 50s \nas well, coldest tonight (Tuesday night) behind a weak frontal \npassage today. We may see a few light showers pop up over the high\ncountry (Absaroka/Beartooth mountains) from time to time, but \nwith little if any impact. Late Thursday into Friday, winds begin \nto increase over the Bighorn mountains ahead of the next \napproaching weather system. With this, southwesterly wind gusts in\nthe 30s to around 50 mph are forecast. While the strongest winds \nlook to remain in the mountains during this time, windy conditions\nmay affect portions of Sheridan County, mainly south and east of \nSheridan. \n\nBy Friday, the aforementioned upper low will have moved across \nthe Rockies and into our region, syncing up with another trough \nmoving across southern Canada. Models suggest an associated cold \nfront will push through our CWA Friday afternoon bringing cooler \ntemperatures, windy conditions, and precipitation to the area.\n \nWhile ensembles are in pretty good agreement regarding the timing, \nthere remains uncertainty with regard to other details. Strong \nupper dynamics and instability may produce pockets or even a \nnorth-south oriented line of moderate rain/mtn snow into Friday \nevening. As far as the wind potential goes, there is currently a \nmedium chance of seeing wind gusts greater than 40 mph across the \nregion late Friday into early Saturday. For precipitation \npotential, the chance of precipitation is greatest over south- \ncentral/western Montana (70+ percent chance of precipitation). \nCannot rule out a little embedded thunder as well. Snow levels are\nprogged to start out around 10,000 feet Friday, dropping to \naround 5,000 feet Saturday...though much of the precipitation will\nhave ended by then. As far as amounts go, the chance of seeing \n0.50 inches of liquid precipitation (rain or melted snow) remains \n50 to 80 percent over south-central Montana, decreasing well below\n50 percent over far southeastern Montana. The chance of seeing six\nor more inches of snow is 50 percent in the Absaroka/Beartooth \nmountains and lower in the surrounding mountain ranges.\n\nCluster analysis suggests the split flow troughs move out for the\nweekend leaving a zonal flow in their wake over our region. Thus,\nexpect a drying trend Saturday into Monday with breezy conditions\nin the mountains and foothills. Daytime highs Temps will be on \nthe cool side, in the 50s for most locations. BT",
